hreshold has to be less  than hundred percent so you can imagine  the threshold could go over a hundred  percent because you have this one point  six multiplier but it's capped at a  hundred and there's actually also a  minimum value so you can't be part of  this program unless you meet the minimum  so this slide has a lot of equations on  it so just bear with me and actually I  will also say that all of this stuff is  on our github account so and it's  actually pretty well documented we have  a an eye Python notebook that walks  through all of this stuff so if you  don't get it here don't worry this is  just to give you like an overview if  you're interested more please go and  look at our github so the goal is just  to maximize the reimbursements which are  basically the number of lunches times  the the price of the lunch multiplied by  the threshold and the same thing for the  number of breakfasts because this  includes breakfasts and lunches for the  kids and the way I've kind of written  out this equation I've just put the  everyone is is reimbursed at the base  rate no matter what so I kind of just  put that off to the side that's just to  make the optimization a little bit  easier to look at but that part doesn't  depend on the groupings so one thing you  can do is you can think about okay how  do we actually group schools and what's  going to be the difference if we group  them versus not group them so that's a  Delta r1 - the difference between having  them grouped so that's going to tell us  if we have two schools whether or not we  should group them or not well it turns  out that you know if you do a little bit  of algebra you can come up with which is  which is interesting because it's what's  important there is a difference in the  threshold so threshold - - threshold one  that that's an important part of it and  then the other important part is m1  minus m2 and I kind of call these M's  they're basically like the meal per  student and this is this is e