od called simulated annealing which  actually lets you take some backward  steps like okay it kind of relaxes that  if your step is bad it kind of still  lets you take it some of the time so the  idea there is that maybe you'll you'll  hit a local global Maxima so it's a very  similar it's a very similar algorithm  the only difference is in your accept  and reject  if it's if delta r is greater than zero  you still always accept but if it's less  than zero then you only accept with a  probability of e raised to Delta R over  T so T is like this made-up parameter  and it's it's essentially you know it's  modeled off physical systems where it be  temperature in this case it doesn't  really mean anything except that we want  to keep doing this and at the same time  we let T go to zero so this is is  essentially what the function looks like  at different values of T so you can see  as you let T go to zero you're again  approximating that step function that  that you know was in in the previous  case right so essentially as as this  goes to zero we're getting back this to  Castaic hill climbing okay so I think  I'm going to hand it over to Simon to  tell us a little bit about how we kind  of implemented this and made it run  faster because we're doing a whole bunch  of these at once I am gonna pull up the  actual code for this part yeah we did  write code just just so everyone knows I  think what's worth pointing out here is  that when we did this originally we just  wrote code off the top of our heads and  we didn't really pay attention to  efficiency and it took a long time on  the order of 11 minutes to do this for I  think a hundred iterations so then you  want to do it over and over and over  again to make sure that you're actually  getting a good stable solution and it's  not like we were writing you know  incredibly stupid like three times  nested for loops or anything it just  took a long time without using the  pandas group by operator which I think  I've been u